WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.180 --> 00:00:05.360
For decades people have been 
fascinated by creatures of the past.

00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:09.600
What did they look like? How did they move?

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:12.760
Many theories have been
developed for many species.

00:00:12.760 --> 00:00:18.800
Now researchers have turned their attention
to an ancient creature called Orobates.

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:22.240
Which lived before the
dinosaurs roamed the earth.

00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:26.580
Using cutting-edge techniques,
from computer modeling to robotics,

00:00:26.580 --> 00:00:29.740
they think they've worked out how it moved.

00:00:41.080 --> 00:00:45.860
Orobates lived around 
280 million years ago.

00:00:45.860 --> 00:00:50.880
It was a time when vertebrates were
still quite new to walking on land.

00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:54.780
But its skeleton has been exquisitely preserved.

00:00:54.780 --> 00:00:57.260
To work out how it might have moved

00:00:57.260 --> 00:01:00.850
researchers scanned the fossil in three dimensions

00:01:00.850 --> 00:01:05.720
and used this to construct a digital
model of the Orobates' skeleton.

00:01:05.720 --> 00:01:08.600
This model could then be animated

00:01:08.600 --> 00:01:11.120
sort of like a digital marionette.

00:01:11.120 --> 00:01:15.880
The marionette helped them
work out the range of motion for each joint

00:01:15.880 --> 00:01:19.720
telling them how far Orobates
could bend or stretch.

00:01:19.720 --> 00:01:22.260
But it's unlikely that Orobates walked

00:01:22.260 --> 00:01:25.340
using its full range of motion in every joint

00:01:25.340 --> 00:01:28.020
so they needed to narrow it down a bit.

00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:31.619
One way to do that is to look at another fossil,

00:01:31.619 --> 00:01:35.960
not of Orobates itself but of its tracks.

00:01:35.960 --> 00:01:40.660
This fossil shows where an Orobates
specimen placed its feet

00:01:40.660 --> 00:01:43.800
long ago as it rambled across the ground.

00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:48.550
Programming the skeleton marionette to
walk inside the fossilized footsteps

00:01:48.550 --> 00:01:51.280
narrowed down its movement further,

00:01:51.380 --> 00:01:53.260
but there are still many options for

00:01:53.260 --> 00:01:56.400
how an Orobates could have made the footprints.

00:01:56.400 --> 00:02:00.700
To narrow it down more researchers
turned to living animals.

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:06.660
The layout of Orobates' skeleton suggests
 it would have had a sprawling gait

00:02:06.660 --> 00:02:09.540
like modern-day lizards or salamanders.

00:02:09.540 --> 00:02:14.800
So researchers used x-ray video
to film four such creatures.

00:02:15.440 --> 00:02:21.320
These data helped them identify four key parameters which define sprawling movement.

00:02:28.060 --> 00:02:32.800
These four parameters were used
as the basis for a dynamic simulation

00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:36.400
containing the skeleton marionette and the footprints.

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:39.420
As the weighting of these four traits is adjusted

00:02:39.420 --> 00:02:43.269
the simulation spits out graphs
containing all the possible movement

00:02:43.269 --> 00:02:45.400
combinations which fit the bill.

00:02:45.400 --> 00:02:51.160
Then the researchers could pick through 
all the outputs to find the most likely options.

00:02:51.920 --> 00:02:53.500
But there's still a catch.

00:02:53.500 --> 00:02:56.480
Computer simulations can't account for everything,

00:02:56.480 --> 00:02:58.780
like the complexities of gravity.

00:02:58.780 --> 00:03:03.760
There are so many variables that it's
really hard to model them all in detail.

00:03:03.760 --> 00:03:09.840
So researchers went one step further to
validate their predictions in the real world.

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:12.960
They built OroBot.

00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:18.420
This scale-sized robot was also modeled 
and added to the dynamic simulation.

00:03:18.420 --> 00:03:21.300
Bringing with it more physical constraints

00:03:21.300 --> 00:03:23.340
narrowing down the options.

00:03:23.340 --> 00:03:26.600
And, combining all of these different approaches

00:03:26.600 --> 00:03:28.900
they settled on an answer.

00:03:28.900 --> 00:03:34.040
The researchers think the Orobates
moved with a relatively upright gait

00:03:34.040 --> 00:03:38.900
and had more advanced capabilities than 
expected for such an early land dweller.

00:03:38.900 --> 00:03:42.020
And that hints at a different ecology.

00:03:42.020 --> 00:03:46.400
A more mobile animal may have been more
adept at picking through the landscape

00:03:46.400 --> 00:03:51.120
and perhaps be able to travel further 
from water than scientists had predicted.

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:53.700
The researchers hope that some of their methods

00:03:53.700 --> 00:03:57.840
could help reverse engineer the movement 
of other fossilized animals

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:01.280
and they've made their model
available for free online.

00:04:01.280 --> 00:04:04.720
Click the link in the description
below to start your own investigation

00:04:04.720 --> 00:04:08.360
into Orobates' movement

